      "title": "sgl-project/sglang: SGLang is a high-performance serving framework for large language models and multimodal models.",
      "url": "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang",
      "snippet": " for LLMs and multimodal models\n\n  \n\nDocs | Cookbook | Website | Blog | Slack\n\nSGLang is an open-source inference framework for large language, vision-language, and diffusion models, optimized for agentic workloads, large-scale serving, and RL rollouts. SGLang Diffusion is its built-in image and video generation engine, included in this repository and the sglang Python package.\n\n👋 Get started below, or meet the community at SGLang Events, including meetups, developer meetings, workshops, and office hours.\n\nSGLang Summit 2026: Meet the builders of open AI\nOur flagship annual gathering · November 12–13 · San Francisco\n\nGo deep on inference, training, and AI hardware through technical talks, live demos, and hands-on workshops. Bring your deployment challenges and meet the engineers and researc … ning, and AI hardware through technical talks, live demos, and hands-on workshops. Bring your deployment challenges and meet the engineers and researchers building the stack.\n\nGet Started\n\nPull the Docker image, which includes SGLang and its dependencies:\n\ndocker pull lmsysorg/sglang:latest\n\nAlternatively, install SGLang in an activated Python environment with uv:\n\nuv pip install --prerelease=allow sglang\n\nNext, launch your model:\n\nQuickstart: Run your first model and send a request.\nCookbook: Choose your model and hardware to get a ready-to-run launch command.\nSupported Hardware\n\nSGLang supports a wide range of GPUs, TPUs, NPUs, CPUs, and Apple Silicon platforms.\n\nPlatform\tRepresentative hardware\nNVIDIA\tB200/B300/GB200/GB300; H100/H200/H800/H20; A100; RTX 30/40/50 series, RTX 6000 Ada / PR … provements.\n\nDevelopment setup\n\nStart from the lmsysorg/sglang:dev Docker image, which provides development tools and most dependencies. Clone or mount your SGLang checkout inside the container, then install it in editable mode from the repository root so tests use your local Python changes:\n\npip install -e \"python\"\n\nIn an activated virtual environment, you can use uv pip install --prerelease=allow -e \"python\" instead. See the development guide for container setup and testing.\n\nContribute\nFork the repository and create a branch for your changes. For larger changes, discuss your proposal in a GitHub issue or on Slack.\nMake your changes, run the relevant tests, and add regression coverage for fixes or new behavior. Run pre-commit run --all-files before submitting.\nOpen a pull request describi",
